 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "Authentication is checking that a user is really who they claim to be before giving access to a device, an account or data.\nThree kinds of factors:\n• something you know: a password, a PIN, a secret question;\n• something you have: a phone that receives a code (OTP), a bank card, a security key;\n• something you are (biometrics): fingerprint, face, voice, iris.\nTwo-factor (multi-factor) authentication uses two different kinds: for example a password and a code sent by SMS — much safer than a password alone.\nAuthorisation comes after authentication: it decides what the user is allowed to do.\nGood practice: strong unique passwords, never share codes, lock your screen, log out on shared computers.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {},
  "problem": "To withdraw money, Bih puts her card in the machine and types her PIN. Which authentication factors are used?",
  "steps": [
   "The card is something she has.",
   "The PIN is something she knows. Two different factors: two-factor authentication."
  ],
  "answer": "Something she has (card) and something she knows (PIN).",
  "checks": []
 },
